  "Question": "The expression \\(x^2-y^2-z^2+2yz+x+y-z\\) has:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "no linear factor with integer coefficients and integer\nexponents",
    "B": "the factor \\(-x+y+z\\)",
    "C": "the factor \\(x-y-z+1\\)",
    "D": "the factor \\(x+y-z+1\\)",
    "E": "the factor \\(x-y+z+1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The terms involving only \\(y\\) and \\(z\\) combine into \\(-(y-z)^2\\). This reveals a difference of squares, whose factor \\(x+y-z\\) also appears among the remaining terms:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x^2-y^2-z^2+2yz+x+y-z\\\\\n&=x^2-(y-z)^2+(x+y-z)\\\\\n&=(x+y-z)(x-y+z)+(x+y-z)\\\\\n&=(x+y-z)(x-y+z+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBoth factors are linear with integer coefficients. In particular, the expression has the factor \\(x-y+z+1\\), so the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
